1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,920
In this lesson, we'll combine what we learned in the previous two lessons and go over how

2
00:00:05,920 --> 00:00:09,820
 to use OpenAI functions with LaneChainExpress and Language.

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:15,480
 We'll also introduce Pydantic, a Python library that makes it easier to construct OpenAI

4
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:16,480
 functions.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,220
 Let's get to it.

6
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:19,220
 So what is Pydantic?

7
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:22,760
 Pydantic is a data validation library for Python.

8
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:26,420
 It makes it really easy to define different schemas, and then it also makes it really

9
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:29,560
 easy to export those schemas to JSON.

10
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:33,880
 That's going to come in handy because we can use the Pydantic objects to create the

11
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:36,240
 OpenAI functions descriptions.

12
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:40,920
 If you remember, those OpenAI function descriptions were a big blob of JSON with a bunch of different

13
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:45,920
 components, and so rather than having to think about all of that, we can use Pydantic.

14
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:49,460
 The way that we'll do that is we'll define a Pydantic class.

15
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,560
 It's very similar to a normal Python class, so you can see them side by side here.

16
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:56,820
 And the main difference is, rather than having an init method, we're just going to list the

17
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:00,580
 attributes and their type under the class definition.

18
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:02,620
 We're not actually going to use these classes for anything.

19
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:07,040
 We're just going to use it to create that OpenAI functions JSON.

20
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:10,580
 Let's take a look at what this looks like in practice.

21
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:14,900
 First we're going to load in all our normal environment variables.

22
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:18,700
 We'll then import a few Pydantic classes as well as some type pins that we'll use later

23
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:19,700
 on.

24
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:23,700
 We'll start with just a normal Python class.

25
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:25,460
 So this is a normal Python class for user.

26
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:31,300
 It has an init method, and we pass in name, age, and email.

27
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:38,180
 So if we create an instance of this, we can see that we have a normal Python class, and we

28
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:42,180
 can access those elements accordingly.

29
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:48,540
 If we pass in an invalid value for age, so here we have bar, which is a string, and we expect

30
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:54,400
 it to be an integer, we can see that it creates it okay.

31
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,600
 And if we look at the element, we have it accessed.

32
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,660
 And that's actually not good, because we do want to have some validation that if we say

33
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:02,620
 it's an integer, it actually is an integer.

34
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,980
 Let's now take a look at doing this with Pydantic.

35
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:09,540
 We can now have our class inherit from base model, and this is imported from Pydantic above.

36
00:02:09,540 --> 00:02:13,540
 And then we can define our attributes just right under the class definition with various

37
00:02:13,540 --> 00:02:14,540
 type pins.

38
00:02:14,540 --> 00:02:19,640
 So we're saying name is a string, age is an integer, email is another string.

39
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:23,860
 We can create an object as normal, and if we inspect this object, we can actually see that

40
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:27,780
 it prints out nicely all the different elements, and so this is a nice benefit of Pydantic as

41
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,780
 well.

42
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:35,780
 And similarly, we can access individual elements like that.

43
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:40,380
 Now if we try to pass in an invalid argument for age, if we try to pass in a string of value

44
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:43,340
 bar, we can see that it raises a validation error.

45
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,460
 So this is something that Pydantic is doing under the hood, it's actually doing some additional

46
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:52,100
 validation on the inputs that we pass in, which is another nice benefit of Pydantic.

47
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:56,700
 One other thing we can do with Pydantic is we can actually nest these data structures.

48
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:01,000
 So here we're going to define a class type, and this is a Pydantic model, so it's inheriting

49
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,500
 from base model.

50
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,320
 And then the only element on this class is going to be students, and students is going to be

51
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,520
 a list of P user, which we defined above.

52
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:14,060
 And now we can create an object that has this exact structure.

53
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:18,700
 So we're passing in a list of students, in this case one, and we're getting an object back.

54
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:23,320
 And if we look at what this object looks like, we can see again that it's printed out nicely.

55
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:26,120
 And so the point here is that you can nest Pydantic objects.

56
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:27,620
 This is a brief introduction to Pydantic.

57
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:31,620
 If you want to learn more, I'd encourage you to go look at their docs.

58
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:35,700
 Or now is also a good time to play around with it a little bit, try out some different type

59
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,820
 hints, see what it ends up looking like.

60
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:44,500
 Now we're going to talk about how to use Pydantic to create these OpenAI function definitions.

61
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:48,920
 So what we're going to do is we're going to create a Pydantic object that we can then cast

62
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,460
 to that JSON schema that we went over in lesson one.

63
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:56,180
 Importantly here, the Pydantic object that we create isn't actually going to do anything.

64
00:03:56,180 --> 00:04:00,300
 We're just using it to create this schema.

65
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:03,740
 We'll create a class called WeatherSearch, and this lines up with the function that we created

66
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:04,900
 earlier.

67
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:06,620
 We're inheriting from base model.

68
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:10,340
 You can see also here that we're adding a doc string, and we'll see how this gets reflected

69
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:11,720
 in later on.

70
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:16,320
 And then we're having a single argument called airport code, which we type in as a string.

71
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:22,440
 And then we also add in this field description for airport code to get the weather for.

72
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:27,100
 Field is a Pydantic module that we can use to set things like the description of various

73
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,400
 fields.

74
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:31,940
 From there, we're going to import a function from linkchain.

75
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:37,080
 From linkchain utils OpenAI functions, import convert Pydantic to OpenAI functions.

76
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:39,180
 This function is going to do exactly what it sounds like.

77
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:43,560
 It's going to convert a Pydantic object to the JSON schema that's expected for an OpenAI

78
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:50,160
 function.

79
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:54,780
 And so if we pass the class in here, and notice that we're passing in the name of the class,

80
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:57,140
 we're not initializing it with an object.

81
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:00,500
 We're passing in the class type, we get back weather function.

82
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:04,740
 If we look at what weather function is, this is a JSON schema in the same form as what we

83
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:07,760
 passed into OpenAI in the first lesson.

84
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:09,520
 We can see that it has name weather search.

85
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,140
 So this is the name of the Python class.

86
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:13,140
 We can also see that it has a description.

87
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:16,680
 And this is pulled from the doc string here.

88
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:22,200
 For parameters, we can see that it has a list of properties, one of which is airport code,

89
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:24,200
 or the only one of which is airport code.

90
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:27,140
 And this is pulled from the arguments that are listed here.

91
00:05:27,140 --> 00:05:31,540
 Airport code itself has a description, and that's pulled from the field description here.

92
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:33,300
 And then it has a type, which is string.

93
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:37,920
 We've made some assumptions about how people would want to create OpenAI functions.

94
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:43,920
 So, one thing in particular that we've done is we've made this doc string here required, because

95
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:46,300
 that gets put in to the description of the function.

96
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:48,920
 And as we talked about earlier, the functions really are prompts.

97
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:52,600
 And so if you're passing in a function, you want to give a good description of what that

98
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,000
 function is doing.

99
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,880
 And so we've added some checks that enforce that you are passing in the description.

100
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:05,260
 So, if we have a separate class called weather search one, and if we call convert pidantic

101
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:12,200
 to open AI function on this, we actually get an error because of this enforcement that we're

102
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:14,880
 using a description.

103
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:17,280
 We don't enforce descriptions for everywhere though.

104
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:22,120
 So if we remove the airport code description and create a new tool called weather search two,

105
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:29,120
 and then we call convert pidantic to open AI function on this, we can see that it succeeds.

106
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:34,540
 Now, however, you'll see that there's no description for the airport code argument.

107
00:06:34,540 --> 00:06:38,300
 Descriptions to arguments are optional in link chain, they're not required.

108
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:43,240
 Let's now look at combining open AI functions with link chain expression language.

109
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:53,480
 So first, let's import the chat model.

110
00:06:53,480 --> 00:06:58,360
 We'll then create an instance of this model.

111
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:01,180
 And for now, we'll just interact with it directly.

112
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:07,780
 So specifically what we're going to do is we're going to call invoke on this model.

113
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:10,160
 Let's ask a question that's going to require the weather function.

114
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:16,460
 So what is the weather in SF today?

115
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:20,340
 And then we can also pass in keyword arguments.

116
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:26,440
 So we'll pass in this weather function that we defined above.

117
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:32,600
 And so here, what we get back from the model is going to be a content message that's null.

118
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:36,880
 And then in the additional quarks field, we're going to have this function call parameter,

119
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:41,180
 which returns a function call with name weather search, and then arguments airport code SFO.

120
00:07:41,180 --> 00:07:43,180
 So it's using the function appropriately.

121
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:48,480
 What we can also do is we can bind the function invocations to the model.

122
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:53,480
 And one reason for doing this is so that we could just pass that model plus functions around

123
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:58,480
 together and not have to worry about always passing in these function keyword arguments.

124
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:10,780
 So here, if we do model with function, and set that equal to model dot bind functions equals weather function,

125
00:08:10,780 --> 00:08:14,780
 we can now call this model with function directly.

126
00:08:14,780 --> 00:08:25,080
 And we can just pass in what is the weather in SF.

127
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:28,080
 And now we can see that it responds and it's still using the function call.

128
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:33,080
 And that's because it still knows that the function calls exist because we've bound them to the model like that.

129
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:45,380
 And so we can do the same thing and we can force the model to use a function.

130
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:49,380
 And so we can bind the weather function as above.

131
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:53,180
 And then we can also bind this function call argument.

132
00:08:53,180 --> 00:09:03,380
 And we set this equal to name and then the value of the function that we want to call, which is weather search.

133
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:10,080
 And now if we call this, we can see that it's again calling the function call.

134
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:15,280
 Again, this is actually expected and desired because it's a question about weather.

135
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:16,480
 And so we should be calling it.

136
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:20,380
 We can maybe see exactly a better representation of this if we just say hi.

137
00:09:20,380 --> 00:09:23,580
 And then we can see that it's still forcing it to call a function.

138
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:26,180
 And it's just calling it with airport code LAX.

139
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:33,580
 We can now use this model, which has the function bound to it in a chain like we'd normally use a model.

140
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:37,780
 And so in order to do that, we're going to import a prompt template.

141
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:54,980
 We'll create a really simple prompt template, so we'll do something like chat prompt template from messages.

142
00:09:54,980 --> 00:10:02,780
 We'll create a really simple system message.

143
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:11,580
 And then we'll add our user input here.

144
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:23,580
 We'll then create a chain by just piping together prompt and the model with the function.

145
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:25,380
 So we'll still let it decide what to do.

146
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:42,780
 And now we can do chain.invoke with input, what is the weather in SF, and it pipes it through the prompt and then to the model at the end.

147
00:10:42,780 --> 00:10:50,880
 Our next step up is we can pass in a list of functions and let the language model decide which one to use based on the question context.

148
00:10:51,880 --> 00:11:01,680
 So here we're going to create another Pydantic model, this one called artist search, and we're going to have the description and the usage be call this to get the names of songs by a particular artist.

149
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:08,780
 We're going to add two arguments, the artist name and then n, which is an integer corresponding to the number of results to look up.

150
00:11:10,780 --> 00:11:18,680
 We'll then create a new list of functions, and this time there's going to be two, weather search and artist search, and we're going to use convert Pydantic to OpenAI function on both of them.

151
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:23,680
 And we're going to create a new object called model with functions.

152
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:26,480
 And we're going to do model.bind.

153
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:30,180
 And we're going to attach the functions here.

154
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:36,080
 And now let's try invoking this with different inputs and see what happens.

155
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:41,380
 So first ask it, what is the weather in SF?

156
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:43,380
 And here it responds.

157
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:45,080
 It's going to do a function call.

158
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:46,980
 It's going to call the weather search tool.

159
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:49,480
 Let's try this again, but let's change it up.

160
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:51,180
 Let's ask it something about music.

161
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:58,680
 So let's say, what are three songs by Taylor Swift?

162
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:06,380
 And here we get is calling the artist search, artist name is Taylor Swift, and then n is equal to three.

163
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:08,380
 So it recognizes that we want three songs.

164
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:11,680
 And again, here we're not forcing it to call a function.

165
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:16,980
 So if we just say, hi, it should respond with something that doesn't use functions at all.

166
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:18,780
 So hello, how can I assist you today?

167
00:12:18,780 --> 00:12:21,680
 Now's a good point to pause and try this out.

168
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:22,080
 Try it.

169
00:12:22,080 --> 00:12:29,980
 Try passing in a few different Pydantic objects to convert Pydantic to OpenAI functions, getting a list of multiple functions, binding those to a model,

170
00:12:29,980 --> 00:12:33,480
 and then passing in various inputs and seeing what the response turns out to be.

171
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:39,380
 That's it for an overview of using link chain expression language and OpenAI functions together.

172
00:12:39,380 --> 00:12:46,680
 In the next lesson, we're going to talk about one of the main use cases for this type of functionality, tagging and extraction.

